<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George IV of Georgia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1191">1191</a>–<a href="/years/1223">1223</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Lasha Giorgi, George IV Lasha · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George IV, known as Lasha Giorgi, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> from 1213 to 1223, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamar-of-georgia">Queen Tamar</a>. He continued his mother's wars against neighbouring Muslim rulers and planned to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-crusade">Fifth Crusade</a>, but in 1221 a Mongol force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> defeated him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-khunan">Khunan</a>. He died in 1223, reportedly of wounds received in the fighting.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-iv-of-georgia.jpg" alt="George IV of Georgia" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domainPublic domainfalsefalse This work is in the public domain in its country of origin and other countries and areas … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giorgi_IV_Lasha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>George IV of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a> was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamar-of-georgia">Queen Tamar</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and her consort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-soslan">David Soslan</a>. His mother declared him co-regent in 1207. According to the Georgian chronicles, his second name Lasha meant illuminator of the world in the Abkhaz language. He had a princely domain in Javakheti, centred on Alastani, and was known as javakht' up'ali, the Lord of the Javakhians, as a type of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coin struck in his name suggests.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Tamar's death George continued her policy of strengthening the feudal Georgian state. His supporters included Sargis Tmogveli, Shalva and Ivane Akhaltsikheli, Sula Surameli and Botso and Memna Jaqeli, while Ivane I Mkhargrdzeli and Varam Gageli opposed him. When the atabeg of Ganja, Muzaffar al-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a>, stopped paying tribute, the Darbazi approved a campaign. The siege went badly, and fighting won by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> caused heavy casualties, but the atabeg agreed to pay tribute again. In 1219 George campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhchivan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahlat">Ahlat</a> and forced them to pay annual tribute.</p><p>In the 1210s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> hurt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> states. An inscription at Ani records that Catholicos Epiphanes ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">tithe</a> cut by two thirds after fees for baptism, marriage and burial had tripled. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> had secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>'s participation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-crusade">Fifth Crusade</a>, and George, according to the chronicles, prepared a campaign in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>. A Mongol expedition defeated two Georgian armies in 1221 and 1222, and the king was severely wounded; the invasion cut off his plans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>George went to Bagavan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> to secure his sister Rusudan's marriage to the Shah of Shirvan and ensure her succession. He died there on 18 January 1223, of complications from his wound. Rusudan succeeded him, and he was buried at Gelati monastery.</p><p>In 1215 a woman from Velistsikhe, married to another man, bore him a son, David VII, who was later king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> from 1246 to 1270. George gave the child to Rusudan to raise. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a>, the Catholicos and ministers confronted him, and the woman was sent back to her husband, but George insisted that she was his wife and refused any other marriage arranged by the court.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George IV of Georgia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Georgia. Died in Bagavan, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.61,43.52&amp;year=1211" aria-label="Bagavan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5624 745 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of George IV of Georgia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6074 1015h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6074 1015h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=39.61,43.52&amp;year=1211">What was here in 1211? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
